messages:   84%|########3 | 503/600 [10:42<00:27,  3.50it/s][["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nWe ought to pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art. Its other, extrinsic properties are irrelevant to our aesthetic interactions with it. For example, when we look at a painting we should consider only what is directly presented in our experience of it. What is really aesthetically relevant, ttherefore, is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is added to the premises?\n# Options:\nA) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.\nB) Only an artwork's intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.\nC) It is possible in theory for an artwork to symbolize nothing.\nD) What an artwork symbolizes involves only extrinsic properties of that work.", "<think>.\nTo determine which option logically follows from the given context, let's analyze each option step by step:.\n\nA) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.\n- This option suggests that there are symbolic properties in our experiences of artworks.However, the context states that we should pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art.This means that any symbolic properties would be extrinsic and irrelevant.Therefore, this option contradicts the context.\n\nB) Only an artwork's intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.\n- This option aligns perfectly with the context, which states that we should consider only the intrinsic properties of a work of art.This option supports the context and does not contradict it.\n\nWhat an artwork symbolizes involves intrinsic properties of that work, not just extrinsic properties.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
